** THE PARALLEL SPEEDUP IS 2.1x, NOT THE ~6x PROJECTED. The projection was wrong; here is why. **
Per-worker nodes at k=5:
  w0 1,065,305   w1 1,130,750   w2   306,684   w3   680,088
  w4 2,184,622   w5 1,604,668   w6 2,358,918   w7 16,573,044
Worker 7 alone holds 64.0% of all 25.9M nodes. run-oracle-parallel.sh shards initex() by first vertex
type, w handling {i : i%N==w}; on this palette that partition is wildly unbalanced. The balance-limited
speedup ceiling is total/max = 1.56x REGARDLESS of core count -- adding workers cannot help, because
one worker is doing two thirds of the search. Measured 2.1x beats that ceiling only because the 1s
timer resolution is too coarse at 8s; the true figure is near 1.6x.
Machine: hw.ncpu 10 but hw.perflevel0.logicalcpu 4 (4 performance + 6 efficiency cores), so even a
balanced shard would not have reached 8x.

Revised projection (serial, 6.3x nodes per k, time tracking nodes):
  k=6 ~1.6e8 nodes / ~1.7min   k=7 ~1.0e9 / ~11min   k=8 ~6.5e9 / ~70min
Parallel as it stands buys ~1.6x on top of that, not 6x. To do better the shard key must change from
i%N to something load-aware (e.g. split the heavy first-type subtree, or greedy bin-pack by a cheap
node estimate). That is a solver-harness change, not a solver change.
